
{{Culture of Balisca}}

The '''Revolutionary calendar''' ([[Baliscano]]: ''calendario revolucionario''), also known as the '''Baliscan calendar''' (''calendario baliscano'') was developed to replace the {{W|Gregorian calendar}} in the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. Having been originally developed in 1828 by the [[Republican Association]], it gained popularity during the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'', and was constitutionally implemented by the delegates of the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. It heavily borrows from the {{W|French Republican calendar}}, which was in use for approximately 12 years, from late 1793 to 1805, and was also briefly used for 18 days by the {{W|Paris Commune}} in 1871.

The revolutionary system aimed to {{W|anti-religion|remove all religious}} and {{W|republicanism|monarchist}} influences from the calendar, as part of a broader effort towards {{W|decimalization}} and {{W|standardization}} in the Confederation. This included {{W|decimal time}} of day, {{W|decimalization of currency}}, and {{W|metrication}}. The Revolutionary calendar has since then been widely adopted for ceremonial purposes at all levels of government. Usage of the Gregorian calendar remains widespread as well.

== Description ==

=== Days ===
The months are divided into three ''decadi'' or "weeks" of ten days each, named simply:
*''unidi'' (first day)
*''dodi'' (second day)
*''tridi'' (third day)
*''cuartidi'' (fourth day)
*''quintidi'' (fifth day)
*''sextidi'' (sixth day)
*''setidi'' (seventh day)
*''uastidi'' (eighth day)
*''novenidi'' (ninth day)
*''decadi'' (tenth day)

=== Months ===
The 12 months, each with 30 days, are grouped by the distinct seasons of the Baliscan climate:

Estazión de las Pluvió (Wet Season):
* ''Pluviós'' (Rainy)
* ''Cherminal'' (Germination)
* ''Ventós'' (Windy)
Estazión de la Verdat (Green Season):
* ''Floreal'' (Flowering)
* ''Humidós'' (Humid)
* ''Frondós'' (Leafy/Lush)
Estazión Sécana (Dry Season):
* ''Solero'' (Sunny/Of the Sun)
* ''Seréno'' (Serene/Calm Seas)
* ''Arenós'' (Sandy)
Estazión d'a Cuelta (Harvest Season):
* ''Frutidoro'' (Fruitful)
* ''Recolecto'' (Harvest)
* ''Comunal'' (Communal/Feast)

